course: world
lesson: 66
title: Deserts, rainforests and grasslands
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A biome is a large region of the Earth with its own climate and its own kinds of plants and animals; which biome a place has depends mostly on how warm it is and how much rain falls there.
explain: A desert gets less than about 250 millimetres of rain a year. Not all deserts are hot or sandy: most are rocky or stony, and Antarctica is the largest desert of all. Hot deserts are scorching by day but can be cold at night, because dry, clear air lets the heat escape into the sky.
explain: A tropical rainforest lies near the equator, is hot all year and gets heavy rain almost every day. It has more kinds of living things than any other land biome, arranged in layers: the dark forest floor, the understorey, the thick roof of leaves called the canopy, and a few giant trees poking above it. Its soil is surprisingly poor, because fallen leaves rot fast and the plants take the nutrients back at once.
explain: Grasslands get too little rain for forests but too much for deserts. The hot savanna of Africa, with scattered trees, feeds zebras, wildebeest and lions; the cooler steppes of Ukraine and Asia and the prairies of North America have very rich black soil, built from grass roots over thousands of years, and are now great farmlands for wheat.
explain: Grass survives grazing and fire because it grows from near the ground, not from its tips: when an antelope bites off the top, or a fire sweeps over, the grass grows again from its base.
fact: A biome is a large region with its own climate, plants and animals.
fact: A desert gets less than about 250 millimetres of rain a year.
fact: Antarctica is the largest desert on Earth; the Sahara is the largest hot desert.
fact: Most deserts are rocky or stony rather than covered in sand dunes.
fact: Hot deserts can be very cold at night because dry clear air lets heat escape.
fact: Tropical rainforests are hot and wet all year and lie near the equator.
fact: Rainforests have more kinds of plants and animals than any other land biome.
fact: The canopy is the thick roof of leaves and branches high in a rainforest.
fact: Rainforest soil is often poor, because nutrients are quickly taken back up by plants.
fact: Grasslands get too little rain for forests but more than deserts.
fact: The savanna is a hot grassland with scattered trees, as in much of Africa.
fact: The steppes of Ukraine have very fertile black soil called chernozem.
exercise: What is a biome? | A large region with its own climate, plants and animals | A single kind of animal that lives alone in a wide open place | A small pond in a garden | A machine that measures rain
exercise: What makes a place a desert? | It gets very little rain, less than about 250 millimetres a year | It is always covered in sand dunes | It is always hot both day and night | It has no living things at all
exercise: Which is the largest desert on Earth? | Antarctica | The Sahara | The Gobi | The Kalahari
exercise: What covers most deserts? | Rock and stones | Deep sand dunes | Thick grass | Shallow lakes
exercise: Why are hot deserts often cold at night? | Dry clear air lets the heat escape into the sky | The Sun moves much farther away from the Earth at night | The sand turns to ice after dark | Cold winds blow in from the poles every night
exercise: Where are tropical rainforests found? | Near the equator | Near the North Pole | On the tops of high mountains | In the middle of hot deserts
exercise: What is the canopy of a rainforest? | The thick roof of leaves high above the ground | The dark floor of the forest covered with dead leaves | A wide river that runs right through the forest | The thick roots that hold the tall trees up in the mud
exercise: Why is rainforest soil often poor? | Nutrients are quickly taken back up by the plants | It never rains on the forest floor because the leaves stop it | The trees grow in pure sand | Animals eat all the soil there
exercise: Which biome has the most kinds of living things on land? | The tropical rainforest | The hot and sandy desert | The frozen polar ice caps | The dry open grassy steppe
exercise: What is a savanna? | A hot grassland with scattered trees | A cold forest of pine trees | A sandy desert without any plants at all | A wet marsh by the sea
exercise: Why is the soil of the Ukrainian steppe so rich? | Grass roots built up humus over thousands of years | Volcanoes covered it with fresh ash every single year | Rivers flood it every week and leave thick mud behind | The sea once covered it and left lots of good salt there
exercise: Why can grass grow back after it is eaten or burned? | It grows from near the ground, not from its tips | Its seeds are hidden in the clouds | It has deep woody trunks like trees under the ground | It does not need sunlight to grow
exercise: Why do grasslands not grow into forests? | There is too little rain for many trees | The soil there is too rich for trees | The winters there are too warm for trees | It is too hot for any tree to live anywhere
exercise: Which is the largest hot desert? | The Sahara | Antarctica | The Arctic | The Amazon
test: A traveller packs only shorts and T-shirts for a week of camping in the Sahara. What problem might he have? | He may freeze at night, when the desert gets cold | He will be soaked by heavy rain every single afternoon | He will need snow boots for the dunes | He will find the days too cold for shorts
test: Which is true about deserts? | A desert is defined by very little rain, so it can be cold | Every desert is hot and full of sand dunes | Deserts get rain almost every day | Nothing at all lives in deserts
test: People cut down a patch of rainforest and plant crops, but after a few years the crops grow badly. Why? | The poor soil quickly loses its few nutrients once the forest is gone | The soil gets too rich for crops | The sunlight becomes too weak | It stops raining in the rainforest forever
test: A monkey, a parrot and a sloth spend almost all their lives high in the trees. In which layer do they live? | The canopy | The forest floor | The roots | The riverbed
test: A field of grass is burned by a fire in summer, yet it is green again a few weeks later. Why? | The grass grows again from its base near the ground | New grass seeds fall down from the sky with the next rain | The heat of the fire turned all the black ash into grass | Animals came and planted new grass there with their feet
test: Why is Ukraine called the breadbasket of Europe? | Its steppe soil is rich black chernozem, good for wheat | It is covered by thick tropical rainforest full of monkeys | It has no winter at all | It is a desert with many oases
test: Two places are equally warm, but one gets 2,000 millimetres of rain a year and the other 100. Which is more likely a desert? | The one with 100 millimetres | The one with 2,000 millimetres | Both are deserts | Neither, because both are warm
test: Lions, zebras and acacia trees are seen on a wide grassy plain in Africa. Which biome is it? | Savanna | Rainforest | Desert | Polar land
test: Why can a rainforest grow so many different kinds of living things? | It is warm and wet all year, so plants grow without stopping | It has the richest soil on Earth | It has long cold winters that kill pests | It gets very little rain
test: why's the desert freezing at night when its boiling all day | Dry clear air lets the heat escape fast at night | The sand sucks the heat down deep at night | Cold winds blow in from the sea every night | The desert is much higher up than other places
test: why are there so many kinds of animals in the rainforest | Rainforests are hot and wet all year, so life thrives there | Rainforests have the richest soil on Earth | Rainforests are too dark for hunters to find prey | Rainforests are cold, so the animals there live longer lives
test: my mate says the biggest desert in the world is the Sahara. true? | No, Antarctica is the largest; the Sahara is the largest hot desert | Yes, the Sahara is the largest desert of any kind | Yes, because deserts must be hot and sandy | No, the Amazon is the largest desert
test: Ava reckons all deserts are just sand dunes everywhere. right? | No, most deserts are rocky or stony | Yes, every desert is covered in sand dunes | Yes, apart from the ones that are covered in ice | No, most deserts are covered in short grass
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a desert? | A region receiving less than about 250 millimetres of rain a year | A region invariably covered in sand dunes | A region invariably hot both by day and by night | A region with more species than any other biome
exam: Identify the largest desert upon the Earth. | Antarctica | The Sahara | The Gobi | The Arabian Desert
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which hot deserts become very cold at night. | Dry clear air permits heat to escape | Sand absorbs heat from the air at night | The wind brings snow from the mountains | The Moon draws away the day's warmth
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the soil of a tropical rainforest. | It is often poor, the nutrients being quickly taken up by plants | It is the most fertile soil in the world, owing to the fallen leaves | It consists solely of black chernozem, as upon the grassy steppes | It is perpetually frozen beneath the surface, as in the northern tundra
